WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.320
- In my last video,

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.380
I went over 10 ways to
quickly ruin your life,

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.068
and it is by far the most

00:00:06.068 --> 00:00:07.200
(censored quack)
depressing video

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.670
I have ever made in my life.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.987
And a lot of you who
watched that video said,

00:00:10.987 --> 00:00:14.010
"Wow, I don't actually
need a tutorial for this.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.070
I see myself in every
single one of those points

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.840
and I don't know what to do.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:19.860
I've been depressed
(tranquil ambient music)

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.060
for a long time,

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.030
or maybe I haven't been
feeling well for a long time,

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.340
and this video just sort of reminds me

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.140
of what a big piece of
(censored beep) I am."

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.740
I literally read that same
comment verbatim 50 times.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.260
So I felt obligated to make this video

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.870
because I want to take each
point from the last video

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.450
and flip it to give you guys some ideas

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.910
to help you rise from the ashes,

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.940
get out of whatever
depressive rut that you're in

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.460
so that you can start
feeling better very quickly.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.400
Step number one is to have a goal.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.820
You need to figure out what
you want from your life.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:54.900
And a lot of the time

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.450
we already kind of know
what we want with our lives,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.330
but it is sort of just
this vague sentiment.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.730
We know that we probably
want to have more money

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:07.050
or we want to have a girlfriend,
or we want to pass school

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.800
or to feel better or to have
big muscles or cool hair.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.080
Like we have all these vague sentiments,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.760
but we haven't really sat down

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.770
and separated the wheat from the chaff.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.570
We haven't figured out what things

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.940
are actually important to us,

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.910
what things get us excited
to wake up in the morning

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:25.290
and we don't really outline

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.230
what kind of person we
want to be specifically.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.990
How do we ideally want to spend our time?

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.140
How much time should we
spend playing Runescape?

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.480
How do we want to feel
on a day to day basis?

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.180
What kind of relationships
do we want to have?

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.540
All of these things are
so important to outline

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.740
specifically, otherwise we
don't really have an aim.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.710
We have all of these
vague sentiments of things

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.750
that we kind of want
and that would be nice,

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.390
but we have absolutely no specific goals

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.630
and absolutely no specific
steps to get there.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.800
And without that aim, we
are by definition, aimless.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.220
So no wonder we kind of
descend into nihilism

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.420
without a goal.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.000
We need goals to strive towards,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.300
and it's in that striving
that we find meaning,

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.520
we find that internal fulfillment

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.110
that we're usually looking for

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.170
when we're spending time
on Instagram and whatnot.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:22.680
We're sort of looking for
this feeling of resolution,

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.230
like you're where you're supposed to be.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.350
You find that feeling
by being on your path

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.120
and you don't know if you're on your path

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.740
if you don't have a goal.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.130
So it is so important to spend some time,

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:38.880
sit down with a pen and paper
and figure out in detail

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.360
what you want your life to
look like one month from now,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.060
one year from now, and
five years from now.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.090
Step number two is step out of the cave.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:52.530
One of the easiest ways by
far to descend into despair

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.850
is to spend all of your time
inside in front of screens

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:59.460
getting sucked into endless algorithms,

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.880
being force fed topics that
you are growing tired of,

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.430
and it seems to be the same
things every single day.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.520
You watch the same videos and
you think the same thoughts.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.060
So it's extremely important
that if you don't like that,

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.170
that you stop spending so
much time inside your house.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.270
You have to step outside of the cave,

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:21.960
let the sunlight and
vitamin D hit your skin,

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.450
go be a person in the world,

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.630
stop spending so much time in isolation.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.371
That's probably one of
the biggest things that

00:03:29.371 --> 00:03:32.760
the past couple of years have taught us is

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.710
spending all of our times
in front of a screen

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.990
isn't exactly good for our mental health.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:40.320
So emerge from the cave
as much as possible.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.710
Let people know your
name, get to know people,

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.330
have people know you.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.160
By being a person in the world,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.999
we stop being so closed in on ourselves,

00:03:49.999 --> 00:03:52.980
our soul becomes this black hole

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.790
that just requires entertainment
and people to validate you.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.180
And we almost draw the
world in towards ourself

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.910
rather than being out there

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:07.710
and radiating this sort
of abundant giving nature,

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.270
as woo-woo as that sounds.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.280
When we spend so much time in isolation,

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.230
we get so self-conscious that
every other external input

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:19.026
is sort of like trying to fill this hole

00:04:19.026 --> 00:04:20.970
that we create in ourselves.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:21.900
Let's stop that.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.600
Get outside of the house,
go talk to somebody.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.540
Stop spending all of your
time in your little cave.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:31.380
As much as you are capable,
go be a person in the world.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.350
Step number three to
dramatically improve your life

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.060
is to take risks.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.710
Life is inherently risky, by
being alive, you risk dying,

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:44.250
by crossing the street, you
risk getting hit by a bus.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.530
There are so many things
that could go wrong

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.080
and probably will go wrong in your life,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:49.913
and a lot of these things

00:04:49.913 --> 00:04:51.540
are completely out of your control.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.330
You can't escape bad
things happening to you.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:55.920
You feel pain anyways.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.000
So why not feel the pain
of trying and failing

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.990
rather than the pain of apathy
and despair and neglect?

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:05.340
This doesn't mean

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.670
spend all of your money
on the latest crypto thing

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:12.090
and hope to get rich
because that's just dumb.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.910
Start off small, try to
strike up a conversation

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:18.360
with the person in the elevator
or the grocery checkout.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.960
You might risk being perceived
as sort of a weird person

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:23.850
that people don't want to talk to,

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.260
but that's a great example

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.050
of a risk that is kind of worth taking

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.570
because even if you are seen as a weirdo

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:32.700
and they literally go, "Ugh,
why are you talking to me?"

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.170
That sort of rejection,

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:36.480
when you voluntarily subject yourself

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.550
and you are aware that that could happen,

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.210
but you do it anyways, that
is an empowering rejection.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:43.470
That's the thing about risk,

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:46.320
when you are aware of
the negative downsides,

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:50.130
but you make a calculated
decision to go for it anyways.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:51.780
When that downside happens,

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.120
you at least respect yourself for trying.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.000
And as you take more risks in life,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.610
as you step outside of your comfort zone,

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.250
you start to get better
at evaluating risk.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.260
You start to be a better judge

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:08.010
of what could go wildly
wrong with very little upside

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.140
versus what has like pretty good upside

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:11.550
and very little downside.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.368
You start to develop resilience,

00:06:13.368 --> 00:06:16.590
you start to develop this
like bounce back nature,

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.600
this anti fragility,

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.080
so it's never too late to
start taking more risks.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:25.110
Obviously don't be dumb,
if you have a family,

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.510
don't bank everything on the stock market,

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.370
especially right now.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.350
Don't quote me on that, it's just a hunch.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:35.389
But like I said, from the
start, life is inherently risky.

00:06:35.389 --> 00:06:40.200
By not taking risks and not
getting comfortable with risks,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.660
that's risky in and of itself.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:44.640
In fact, it's worse than risky

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.350
because not only do you risk

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.600
not getting what you
eventually want in life,

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.100
you pretty much guarantee it.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:53.250
So if you're starting to feel
sort of apathetic in life,

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:54.750
stop sitting at the shore,

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:57.030
living in fear for the next big wave

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.820
to get all of your
clothes wet, learn to surf

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:03.060
and expect to be bad at the
start, but you'll get better.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.680
The fourth thing that you can do

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.600
to dramatically improve your life

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.310
is to get out of your head.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.140
Assuming you followed step one

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:11.910
and you developed a clear vision

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:13.350
of what you want with your life

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:16.260
and the things that you
typically want to do each day,

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:19.170
then you've already done
plenty amount of thinking.

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:20.910
So often in our lives

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:23.550
we think that we need
to think things through

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:24.969
in order to do something,

00:07:24.969 --> 00:07:28.890
but like for 99% of the
things, we really do not.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:30.090
Like, if you really feel like

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:31.980
you should be getting
back into the gym again

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.980
and you start to feel inspired
because you watch this like,

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.888
I don't know, workout
montage transformation video

00:07:37.888 --> 00:07:39.300
where like, "Oh, yeah man,

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.130
I really should start
going to the gym again."

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:44.520
so you start looking up
new workout clothes to buy,

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:47.754
you start spending money on
this like advanced YouTuber

00:07:47.754 --> 00:07:49.770
fitness influencer program.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.660
You spend all your money at GNC.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.510
It's like you don't need
to do this much thinking,

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.520
just go to the (censored beep) gym.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.890
Do the thing before you
can even think about it,

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.560
because as soon as you start
thinking, you already know,

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:04.050
you're an absolute master at finding a way

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:05.130
to weasel out of it.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.890
Even if the rationale is fairly cohesive,

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.170
the result is that you
didn't end up doing it.

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:12.450
So many things in life are like this.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:15.270
If you need to write your final essay,

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:16.770
as soon as you think about it

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:18.450
and you already know it's good for you,

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.190
you need to become efficient

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:22.740
at just putting one foot
in front of the other

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:24.150
and already start doing it.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.030
Like literally shut your brain off

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:28.050
and start thinking about the essay

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.750
when you've already written
like six words for it.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:34.650
99% of us have a problem of overthinking,

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:35.790
not under thinking.

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:38.220
Step number five is produce.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:41.079
Always produce more than you consume.

00:08:41.079 --> 00:08:44.340
And in this highly consumeristic culture,

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:46.470
that is very challenging,

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.080
especially if you are
like most people nowadays,

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:50.910
including myself, if I'm not careful.

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:55.020
So many of us sort of sit
there living life passively.

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.660
You know, we're living life on our heels

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:59.760
willingly hypnotizing us

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:02.730
with whatever content is displayed for us

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:06.030
when we enter in YouTube, you
know, maybe that's this video.

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:07.590
Maybe you've been incessantly watching

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:08.940
self-improvement content.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.140
Click away from this video.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.280
You don't need this video.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:12.510
Like come back to it later,

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.340
go do the thing that
you're supposed to do.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:18.030
You know, go produce, stop
consuming content like this.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:19.170
If it's the end of the day

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:21.570
and you're kicking back
and having a good time,

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:23.220
then maybe keep on watching.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.620
But I'm literally gonna screw myself

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:27.240
with this video algorithm wise

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:28.500
because I genuinely mean it.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:30.546
Go do something more important.

00:09:30.546 --> 00:09:33.085
Life feels so much more exciting

00:09:33.085 --> 00:09:37.260
when you are creating more
than you're consuming.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.960
If you like watching
videos, learn to make them.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.506
If you like scrolling
Instagram, looking at photos,

00:09:42.506 --> 00:09:43.830
learn to take them.

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:46.590
The satisfaction that we're longing for

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:50.670
when we're doom scrolling
is the feeling of creation.

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:52.440
It's being in the flow state,

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:55.170
feeling like you are where
you're supposed to be

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.480
and you're doing something significant.

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:59.490
You're putting something
out into the world.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:03.900
So always try to produce at
least as much as you consume,

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:05.190
preferably more.

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:08.250
Step number six is to welcome feedback.

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:09.990
Don't just surround yourself

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:12.840
with people who think
exactly who you think

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:15.660
and you have like three or
four people that you talk to

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:17.970
and you all have the same
opinions about everything

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:20.790
and you look with judgment on people

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:23.940
who live life differently
because your world is so small,

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:25.290
that's pretty much everybody.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:28.050
It's important to have an
inner circle, absolutely.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:30.030
And a lot of the time your inner circle

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:33.090
will think a lot like
you think, absolutely.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:36.780
But surround yourself with
people who challenge you,

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:39.030
who wanna hold you to a higher standard.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.670
If you start to stray off the path,

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:43.170
they can bring you back onto it.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:45.870
And a lot of the time that
won't be very comfortable,

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:48.180
a lot of the time that'll
be a hit to the ego.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:49.650
But you want to find people

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:53.167
who want the best for
the best version of you.

00:10:53.167 --> 00:10:56.850
Not just people who want you
to be comfortable all the time.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:58.350
Try to reflect on the fact

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:00.810
that you probably don't know everything

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.270
and there's a lot to
learn from other people.

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:05.190
This doesn't mean to be spineless

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:07.620
and not stand up for
what you think is right,

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:10.620
but do it in a way that
is solution oriented,

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:12.240
not ego oriented.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:13.770
You do not know everything.

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:15.870
Number seven is be vulnerable.

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:17.407
Now a lot of you might say,

00:11:17.407 --> 00:11:19.680
"That one sounds pretty
beta to be honest with you.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:21.330
That sounds super beta.

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:23.250
Joey's a beta, sure."

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:27.270
By being vulnerable, I don't
mean expose your vital organs

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.120
to a dirty bandit with a sharp knife.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:34.080
I mean, don't be afraid to
be emotionally vulnerable,

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:36.420
emotionally honest, to your inner circle,

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:39.150
to the people who are holding
you to a higher standard.

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:41.893
Now what does this mean and
what's the utility of this?

00:11:41.893 --> 00:11:43.980
Well, let's look at the alternative,

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.770
the alternative is to be secretive,

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.290
to have things that you're ashamed of

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.150
that you never tell anybody.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:53.820
You stuff it down, you
pretend it doesn't exist.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:57.390
And a lot of the time when you
have bad habits or addictions

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:59.880
or something like that
and you keep them hidden,

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:02.455
they tend to blossom in the shadows.

00:12:02.455 --> 00:12:04.650
Right, they start to weigh on you

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.780
far more than they probably should.

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:08.880
When you don't expose your flaws

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:10.890
and your darker side to anybody,

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:13.950
when you're not emotionally
vulnerable and honest,

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:15.960
you know, no one really knows who you are.

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:19.470
You conceal a part of
yourself from the world.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:21.270
Reflect on your life.

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:24.900
Are there things that you
do behind closed doors

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:28.080
that you wouldn't do if
that door were to swing open

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.089
and grandma was home?

00:12:30.089 --> 00:12:31.890
That's a very weird way to put it.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:34.140
And if there are things that we do

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:36.540
that we don't want to
admit to other people,

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:40.380
it's important to find the
courage to let somebody know,

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:41.760
somebody that you trust.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:43.650
This is a very Christian idea actually.

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:48.650
Confession isn't for God or for the priest

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:52.920
or for a person that you're
confiding in, it's for you.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:57.390
It's to shed light on the
darker aspects of yourself

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:00.510
to sort of like heal it,
to like purge it in a way,

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:04.920
'cause these darker sides of
ourselves thrive in secrecy.

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:08.430
Don't be afraid to confide
in somebody that you trust

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:10.857
about things that you're not so proud of

00:13:10.857 --> 00:13:13.650
because a weight will
come off your shoulders

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:14.820
every single time.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:16.200
It's like cathartic.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:19.080
And then you can live your
life with more freedom

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:22.260
to sort of pursue the
higher version of yourself.

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:24.180
Where do you find these types of people?

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:25.350
Outside of your house.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:26.940
Thing number eight,

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:29.578
to dramatically improve
the rest of your life,

00:13:29.578 --> 00:13:33.000
this one's probably the hardest one to do,

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.460
it's the one that I
struggle with the most,

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:38.130
and that is to embrace conflict.

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:39.750
Now this one might sound interesting,

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:43.350
but the alternative is
to run from conflict.

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:45.000
If you have a problem with something,

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:46.770
let somebody know, right.

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:48.780
Otherwise, you're an absolute doormat.

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:52.020
If someone wrongs you, you know,
does something to hurt you,

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.840
does something out of line
that you don't appreciate,

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:56.490
don't just be like, "Oh no, it's okay."

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:58.950
Because if it's not okay, it's not okay,

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:00.780
don't say that it's okay.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:03.570
Because by doing that, you look weaker,

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:06.050
so that person is more likely
to take advantage of you

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:06.883
in the future.

00:14:06.883 --> 00:14:09.480
And they say like, "Wow, this
guy's an absolute doormat."

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:13.650
But far worse is that you
don't respect yourself as much.

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:16.500
You say like, "Wow, I just
let people walk all over me

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.510
because I don't want to deal

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:22.080
with slight discomfort in the short term."

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:24.000
That's something I
struggle with personally.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:27.330
You know, I'm a pretty agreeable
person a lot of the time,

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:29.100
I wanna make sure everyone's comfortable

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:30.270
and having a good time.

00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:33.810
And I have fairly thick
skin, I played hockey

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:38.730
and dressing room culture
in small town Canada,

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:40.200
people throw jabs.

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:43.140
And I don't think that
like being overly sensitive

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:47.520
is particularly beneficial
in that circumstance.

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:50.070
You do have to let stuff kind
of bounce off a little bit,

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:53.156
but when something crosses
the line, you know it.

00:14:53.156 --> 00:14:55.530
And a lot of the time those
aren't like little jabs

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:56.997
or you know humor,

00:14:56.997 --> 00:15:01.200
a lot of the time that's just
like straight up disrespect.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:03.720
They say something about
a family member of yours

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:07.980
or at work, something genuinely
unfair or unprofessional

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:09.720
or unsafe happens.

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:11.190
Don't just be like, "That's all right,

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:12.180
yeah, like don't worry about it."

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:13.140
But yeah.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:14.970
You have to speak up, right?

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:16.620
Otherwise you don't have a spine.

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:17.850
And if you've gotten in the habit

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:19.920
of doing this over years
and years and years,

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:22.710
you almost have to like
re-earn your spine again.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:24.810
And that's tough, that's really tough.

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:27.000
It's something that is extremely important

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:28.980
because that goes along with honesty.

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:30.630
Speak the truth.

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:33.120
If you don't like something,
say that you don't like it.

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.616
Be willing to risk

00:15:34.616 --> 00:15:39.570
people perceiving you as
mildly unpleasant in the moment

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:41.679
in order to gain respect,

00:15:41.679 --> 00:15:43.590
not just from the people around you,

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:45.300
but respect for yourself.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.180
When the elephant in the
room comes barging in,

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:49.470
talk about the elephant,

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:51.270
be like, "Hey, look at
that elephant right there.

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:52.590
What are we gonna do about it?

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:54.297
The thing's (censored beep) huge."

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:57.690
Don't have elephants in your room.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:15:59.010
They don't belong in your room,

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:00.930
they belong in the zoo or in the wild.

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:02.400
Actually, if I say they belong in the zoo,

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:03.390
I'll get canceled.

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:04.330
They belong

00:16:05.256 --> 00:16:07.256
(sighs)

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:09.630
elsewhere.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:12.660
Step number nine, don't play Runescape.

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:14.850
I put that tip in the video

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:17.730
and it's like the number
one comment is like Lamel,

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:19.830
I died at play Runescape.

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:21.960
I played a lot of Runescape, like a lot.

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:24.330
I think I should change it in the video

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:26.730
to play Runescape three,

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:29.100
because old school Runescape still slaps.

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:31.980
All right, tip number 10 is be optimistic.

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:34.380
I think optimism gets a bad rap

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:36.637
because people will look at
optimism and they'll say,

00:16:36.637 --> 00:16:38.910
"Man, this person is not based.

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:39.900
He doesn't realize

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:41.670
that there's a lot of
suffering in the world,

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:44.610
and it's not all sunshine and rainbows."

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:47.100
But unless I have a different definition

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:52.100
of what optimism is, optimism
is the opposite of pessimism.

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:54.960
Pessimism is where you
look at any situation

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:56.347
and you find the negative in it.

00:16:56.347 --> 00:16:58.980
You know, the glass is always half empty.

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:00.840
If you are a pessimist,

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:03.756
you compulsively put a
negative spin on things.

00:17:03.756 --> 00:17:06.780
You have a habit of
looking at anything good

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:11.280
or neutral or bad and finding
the fault in all of it.

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:13.410
And while that might be true,

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:15.510
because the fabric of our reality

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:18.270
is inherently tainted in a lot of ways,

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:19.980
suffering is a part of life.

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:21.379
Everyone's aware of that,

00:17:21.379 --> 00:17:24.527
but why would you add to the suffering

00:17:24.527 --> 00:17:28.800
by being compulsively negative,
by finding a way to complain

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:31.470
or lament about all these
things you have to do?

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:33.936
You're basically cutting
yourself off at the knees

00:17:33.936 --> 00:17:35.910
before you can get going.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:39.540
There's no shortage of
suffering in the world.

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:40.923
You know, life is hard.

00:17:42.458 --> 00:17:46.140
People on motorbikes could ruin your shot

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:49.350
as you're trying to do
a video at 11:00 PM,

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.360
when you have to wake up at
5:00 AM to catch a flight

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:53.850
to London, "Oh, life is so
hard, I'm going to London."

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:57.000
If you have a tendency to compulsively

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.001
look for the negative in everything,

00:17:59.001 --> 00:18:03.150
you add to the net suffering of your life,

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:07.440
it doesn't improve your life
to be overly negative, right.

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:10.410
Seek to find the good in everything

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:11.640
rather than the negative.

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:14.160
That's what being an
optimist is all about.

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:17.340
It's not being blind
to the negative aspects

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:19.560
of the reality around you,

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.740
it's choosing to focus on what's good.

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:25.860
There's a reason why gratitude journals

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:29.640
have been shown to cure in a lot of people

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:32.970
clinical anxiety or insomnia, right.

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:34.950
There's some really
interesting studies on that,

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:37.296
and it's because people focus

00:18:37.296 --> 00:18:40.260
on the positive aspects of their lives

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:42.159
and the things that they're grateful for.

00:18:42.159 --> 00:18:45.780
It doesn't mean that they
like created anything new,

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:48.180
they're just remembering
things differently

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:51.270
and they feel better
about their lives, right.

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:54.660
They have a internal piece
that they're fostering

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:55.830
because they're able

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:59.985
to habitually see the
positive aspect of things.

00:18:59.985 --> 00:19:03.690
And there's the motorcycle
again, I love the motorcycle

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:06.660
because it reminds me of
what an amazing job I have.

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:08.130
Do I actually think that?

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:11.673
I don't know, but I feel
good rather than negative.

00:19:13.836 --> 00:19:16.590
And if you're really good
at being a pessimist,

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:20.130
it's really difficult
to actually recognize

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:23.250
when you're doing it because
you do it so regularly

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:26.340
and it's such a
intellectual slight of hand

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:28.560
that it's really hard to
pick up a lot of the time.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:31.050
This is a very difficult thing to do

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:34.980
to remain optimistic in times of trial.

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:38.310
It's not easy, but it is deeply meaningful

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:42.600
and is deeply satisfying
to try to be that person.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:44.910
So try to recognize all the little ways

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:48.360
that you are being negative
about your yourself,

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:52.710
being negative about your
capabilities, about your future,

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:55.230
about your past, your present.

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:58.920
Try to find all little ways
that you're spinning little fun,

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:00.810
little spiderwebs into negativity,

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:02.520
I don't know how that
makes sense, it doesn't,

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:03.960
but neither does being negative.

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:06.905
So if you've made it to
the end of this video,

00:20:06.905 --> 00:20:09.510
try that today for the rest of the day

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:11.580
or make a note of it tomorrow.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:14.867
Be extra mindful about the
stories you tell yourself

00:20:14.867 --> 00:20:18.030
about the world around you
and about your own life.

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:20.676
Is this a hopeful story

00:20:20.676 --> 00:20:24.510
or is this an overly
bleak and negative story

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:26.850
with no aim and no encouragement?

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:29.100
The world isn't a very encouraging place,

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:30.300
and the last person

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:33.810
that should be robbing you
of encouragement is yourself.

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:36.600
Your life gets better
when you're optimistic.

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:38.100
I will die on that hill.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:41.430
If you have been not feeling so good,

